  clock: 2  # 8fbc
  nonce: 8fbc  # 8fbc
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-08-24 (full text on WI-lutuh): \"yes — WI-lutuh is the long pole and needs scoping now; items 1-3 are prerequisites that make its output readable but don't substitute for it.\" This item is one of those three prerequisites, and the order the owner set is INV-juvul -> INV-rilit -> WI-nakut -> WI-lutuh scoping. The ruling also settles a scope question the project had never made explicitly: verify-claims IS meant to be usable on non-python repositories, which is what promotes WI-lutuh (14 of 15 catalogued languages declare ZERO module_completeness) from a rust footnote to the long pole."  # 8fbc
- op: discuss  # c1bf
  at: '2026-08-24T21:03:36Z'  # c1bf
  by: agent  # c1bf
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c1bf
  clock: 3  # c1bf
  nonce: c1bf  # c1bf
  message: "ROOT CAUSE FOUND 2026-08-24, AND IT IS BIGGER THAN THIS ITEM. Filed as INV-divuf\n(P1), which now blocks this one.\n\nTHE EMPTY NAME IS NOT A CAVEAT-RENDERING BUG. This item correctly ruled out the\nobvious objc-selector explanation (\"dst.split(':')[3] is createFileAtPath, not\nempty\") -- that check was right, and the truncation it shows is real but is a\nSECOND defect. The EMPTY string comes from further upstream:\n\n  ir._parse_dangling_id (line 1523) returns\n      parts[0], symbol_path_slot(dangling_id), parts[-2], parts[-1]\n  -- the PATH half routes to the span-anchored chokepoint, the NAME half is\n  hand-rolled as parts[-2]. An objc selector ENDS with a colon, so parts[-2] is\n  the EMPTY STRING. That value feeds _dedupe_key(language, path, name), so\n  distinct entities collapse into ONE boundary node.\n\nMEASURED on Mantle (31 .m files, unmodified upstream):\n    external dsts                       319 (94 distinct)\n    dsts whose parsed name is EMPTY     268   (84%)\n    distinct names, symbol_name_slot     80\n    distinct names, parts[-2]            17   (79% of identity LOST)\n    one node objc:external:0-0::external_symbol absorbs 49 edges /\n      134 distinct call sites\n    id_format violations                 71\n\nThe \"[warn] 1 id_format validation violation(s)\" this item recorded as \"a lead\nand not a diagnosis\" was exactly the right instinct: the malformed id is\nobjc:external:0-0::external_symbol, and it is the empty name slot.\n\nWHAT REMAINS SCOPED TO THIS ITEM: the JAVA half. The unresolved callee name\ncarries the receiver prefix (u.mkdirs rather than mkdirs) so the\nboundary-scoped untyped_receiver caveat cannot match java.io.File.mkdirs. That\nis a producer-side naming question, not an id-parsing one, and INV-divuf does\nnot cover it.\n\nALSO NOT COVERED BY INV-divuf: whether fixing java's prefix would let the SCOPED\ncaveat fire and thereby ATTRIBUTE the site to a boundary (the more useful\ndisclosure) rather than only counting it -- still not measured, as this item\nalready says."  # c1bf
- op: discuss  # f2ce
  at: '2026-08-24T22:33:36Z'  # f2ce
  by: agent  # f2ce
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f2ce
  clock: 4  # f2ce
  nonce: f2ce  # f2ce
  message: "OBJC HALF RE-RUN ON THE MERGED TREE 2026-08-24 -- AND IT STILL REPRODUCES.\nCorrecting my own handoff, which recorded this half as discharged by PR #511.\nIT IS NOT. Artifacts + minimal repo: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/nakut_closure_08242026/\n\nThis item's filed symptom, verbatim, on dev @dfe3ba8b3e:\n\n  ! [NO-FSWRITE] Verdict: confirmed_with_caveats\n    CAVEAT (unknown_receiver_scope): ... At 1 of 1 method call site(s) the\n    receiver's type could not be determined ... distinct method(s): .\n\nWHY #511 DID NOT FIX IT. #511 converted the READ in unknown_receiver_scope to\nsymbol_name_slot, and that conversion is correct. But the name is gone BEFORE\nverify_claims ever sees the edge:\n\n  analyzer emits           objc:external:0-0:writeToFile:atomically::unresolved\n  _parse_dangling_id name  ''                        <- hand-rolled parts[-2]\n  symbol_name_slot would   'writeToFile:atomically:'\n  _dedupe_key              ('objc', 'external', '')\n  survey carries           objc:external:0-0::external_symbol\n\nAn objc selector ENDS with a colon, so parts[-2] is the empty string.\nir._parse_dangling_id (ir.py:1555) routes the PATH half to the chokepoint and\nhand-rolls the NAME as parts[-2] -- exactly INV-divuf's filed root_cause, and\nthe REDUCED #511 did not convert that site.\n\nTHE PROCESS LESSON (LIVE.md rule 5), because it is the reusable part:\ntest_the_caveat_reports_the_whole_selector PASSES and is not wrong -- it feeds\nunknown_receiver_scope a synthetic PRE-remap edge. Production hands it a\nPOST-remap node whose name slot is already empty. Unit-green did not mean the\npipeline works, and the layer the test skipped is precisely the layer that eats\nthe name. I asserted the discharge from the green test rather than from the CLI.\n\nNOT FIXING IT HERE -- IT IS INSIDE THE INV-divuf OWNER FORK. Converting\n_parse_dangling_id to symbol_name_slot recovers the selector AND keeps entities\ndistinct in _dedupe_key, but the re-minted boundary node id would then carry a\nraw colon in its NAME slot, which ADR-0036 Ruling 1 forbids. So this half is\narm A vs arm B, unsettled, and both arms need an ADR edit.\n\nSTATUS unchanged (todo_soft). Both halves now open with behavioural evidence:\nobjc (above) and java (receiver prefix u.mkdirs cannot match java.io.File.mkdirs\n-- not re-measured this session, unchanged from the filing)."  # f2ce
- op: discuss  # d7cb
  at: '2026-08-25T02:48:17Z'  # d7cb
  by: agent  # d7cb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d7cb
  clock: 5  # d7cb
  nonce: d7cb  # d7cb
  message: "OBJC HALF DISCHARGED 2026-08-24 — behavioural evidence on the MERGED tree\n(dev @f21a8e67ba, PR #515). This item's own filed repro, re-run:\n\n  BEFORE  CAVEAT (unknown_receiver_scope): ... distinct method(s): .\n  AFTER   CAVEAT (unknown_receiver_scope): ... distinct method(s):\n          writeToFile:atomically:\n\nThe count was always right; the NAME was the defect this item filed (\"a reader\nwho sees createFileAtPath can check their own code, a reader who sees '.'\ncannot\"). It now names the whole selector. Repro:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/nakut_closure_08242026/ ; measurements:\n../divuf_fix_08242026/README.md\n\nROOT CAUSE, confirming what this item declined to guess at. This item explicitly\nruled out the obvious explanation (\"dst.split(':')[3] is createFileAtPath, not\nempty\") and said so rather than pretending. That was RIGHT, and the truncation\nit found was a real SECOND defect. The empty string came from further out: the\nboundary node is synthesised FROM the id, an objc selector ENDS in a colon so\nthe id's second-to-last token is '', and ADR-0036 Ruling 1's designated lossless\nhome (Symbol.name) was NEVER FILLED -- measured on the repro as name='',\nqualified_name=None, dst_ref=None, with the selector appearing nowhere in the\noutput. Fixed by filling that home (INV-divuf), not by parsing the id harder.\n\nNOTE ON MY OWN EARLIER CLAIM: I recorded this half as discharged by PR #511 and\nthat was WRONG -- #511 fixed the READ while the name was already destroyed\nupstream. The correction and its mechanism are in this thread; the fix is #515.\n\nJAVA HALF REMAINS AND THIS ITEM STAYS OPEN. The unresolved callee name carries\nthe receiver prefix (u.mkdirs rather than mkdirs), so the boundary-scoped\nuntyped_receiver caveat cannot match java.io.File.mkdirs and only the unscoped\nunknown_receiver_scope caveat fires. NOT re-measured this session. Note that\n#515 does NOT fix it and may make it easier to fix: meta['callee_name'] now\ncarries whatever the java producer passed, so the prefix question is now visible\nin one place rather than inferred from an id.\n\nALSO STILL NOT MEASURED, as this item already said: whether fixing java's prefix\nwould let the SCOPED caveat fire and thereby ATTRIBUTE the site to a boundary\n(the more useful disclosure) rather than only counting it."  # d7cb
